WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.268 --> 00:00:05.903
You may have heard the phrase

00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:09.440
"in space, no one can hear you scream."

00:00:09.473 --> 00:00:10.875
(Laughter)

00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:13.077
But that's true only for human ears.

00:00:13.878 --> 00:00:18.482
The universe is filled with tenuous
atmospheres of one kind or another,

00:00:18.482 --> 00:00:20.785
and through those atmospheres,
pressure waves --

00:00:20.818 --> 00:00:22.920
sound -- moves.

00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:27.591
Through stars, across galaxies,
even between galaxies.

00:00:27.992 --> 00:00:29.226
One of the most wonderful

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.196
and recently discovered
examples of cosmic sound

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:36.434
is in the very young universe,
shortly after the Big Bang.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:40.104
Now you may be wondering,
how would we know that?

00:00:41.138 --> 00:00:44.241
Well famously, as you look
very far out into space,

00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:46.577
you also look back in time,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.847
simply because it takes time
for the light to get to you.

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:55.619
Remarkably, if you look far
enough past all the galaxies,

00:00:55.653 --> 00:00:59.523
you can see to a time
before any galaxies had yet formed,

00:00:59.557 --> 00:01:04.862
even to a time when the universe
itself had only just been born.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.699
So what do you see coming
from this newborn universe?

00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:14.071
You see the light of the Big Bang's
hot, glowing youth.

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:18.976
At that time, the universe was filled
with an almost uniform,

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:20.911
hot, glowing gas

00:01:20.911 --> 00:01:25.349
of atomic nuclei and electrons
and intense light.

00:01:25.916 --> 00:01:31.589
That light, on its way to us,
has crossed an expanding universe.

00:01:31.956 --> 00:01:35.659
That expansion not only carried
those regions far away from us,

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.698
but also as the light waves
crossed an expanding space,

00:01:40.731 --> 00:01:44.135
they were stretched
from micron-sized waves

00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:47.438
to millimeter-sized waves.

00:01:47.738 --> 00:01:51.475
So although it is light
that leaves the young universe,

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:54.078
it’s microwaves that arrive.

00:01:54.578 --> 00:01:58.015
And they are the famous
cosmic microwave background.

00:01:58.716 --> 00:02:01.886
Now because every direction
in which you look

00:02:01.919 --> 00:02:04.388
ends back in the young universe,

00:02:04.388 --> 00:02:08.893
then the microwave radiation
comes to us from all directions,

00:02:08.926 --> 00:02:10.094
and for many years,

00:02:10.127 --> 00:02:13.297
its brightness was thought
to be extremely uniform,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.333
around the full spherical dome of the sky,

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.536
[a] bit like this image here.

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:22.072
But as microwave telescopes
became more sensitive,

00:02:22.106 --> 00:02:26.644
they began to see slight variations
in brightness from place to place,

00:02:26.677 --> 00:02:30.281
so more recent images of the microwave sky

00:02:30.314 --> 00:02:34.084
appear completely covered
in very slight patches.

00:02:34.718 --> 00:02:36.120
You can see them more clearly

00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:39.657
in this blowup of a region
that's about the size of your fist,

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.292
held at arm's length.

00:02:41.692 --> 00:02:46.263
It spans eight million light-years
and shows us the newborn universe

00:02:46.297 --> 00:02:49.466
when it was only 400,000 years old.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.704
Now that's equivalent
to a one-day-old human.

00:02:54.205 --> 00:02:56.307
So what are these patches?

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.244
Why is it slightly brighter and darker
in different places?

00:03:00.644 --> 00:03:02.913
Well, these are places
where the temperature

00:03:02.947 --> 00:03:07.117
and pressure in the glowing gas
are slightly higher and slightly lower,

00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:08.519
or stated differently,

00:03:08.552 --> 00:03:11.555
the bright and dark patches
are the peaks and troughs

00:03:11.555 --> 00:03:13.290
of huge sound waves

00:03:13.324 --> 00:03:16.060
moving through the primordial atmosphere.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.763
The brightness contrast
reveals 90 decibels --

00:03:19.797 --> 00:03:22.466
that's rock-concert loudness,

00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:26.303
and their gigantic size
reveals a frequency or pitch,

00:03:26.337 --> 00:03:30.541
a whopping 50 octaves
below the human range.

00:03:31.008 --> 00:03:36.046
One wave might pass you by
in 50,000 years.

00:03:36.547 --> 00:03:39.250
So what causes this sound?

00:03:39.283 --> 00:03:41.352
Now don’t be fooled.

00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:44.154
It's not the bang of the Big Bang.

00:03:44.188 --> 00:03:48.325
No, it's a slowly growing sound
driven by gravity.

00:03:48.892 --> 00:03:52.463
The distribution of matter
at that time was slightly uneven,

00:03:52.496 --> 00:03:54.231
and where there was a denser region,

00:03:54.231 --> 00:03:57.034
its stronger gravity pulled
in the surrounding gas,

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:59.503
which compressed
and bounced back out again,

00:03:59.536 --> 00:04:01.805
only to fall back in again,

00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:05.442
creating an approximately
spherical sound wave.

00:04:05.843 --> 00:04:09.513
Now the landscape included
small and medium and large regions,

00:04:09.513 --> 00:04:13.317
and so, a bit like a set
of organ pipes of different sizes.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.787
Together, they create
a wide range of pitch.

00:04:17.087 --> 00:04:20.724
So what did the primordial
sound sound like?

00:04:21.358 --> 00:04:24.895
Well to find out, you must first
measure the sounds spectrum.

00:04:25.396 --> 00:04:27.131
Now a computer can do that.

00:04:27.164 --> 00:04:29.566
And here is the remarkable result.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.104
There is a fundamental tone
and several higher harmonics.

00:04:34.104 --> 00:04:35.873
And just for comparison,

00:04:35.906 --> 00:04:39.677
here is the sound spectrum
of a flute playing a single note

00:04:39.677 --> 00:04:42.579
with its fundamental and harmonics.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:48.218
Now although these two sets of harmonics
arise for somewhat different reasons,

00:04:48.252 --> 00:04:52.222
nevertheless, it does seem
that the young universe had qualities

00:04:52.256 --> 00:04:57.494
similar to a musical instrument
or even a human voice singing.

00:04:57.528 --> 00:05:01.498
But notice how those cosmic
harmonics are a little bit broad.

00:05:01.932 --> 00:05:06.570
So please don't expect
the primordial sound to be too clean

00:05:06.603 --> 00:05:09.239
or musical to your human ears.

00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:11.308
Now before I play you the sound,

00:05:11.308 --> 00:05:15.112
take a look at that green line
that goes right through all the data.

00:05:15.145 --> 00:05:17.614
It comes from a detailed
computer calculation

00:05:17.648 --> 00:05:20.017
that aims to include
all the relevant physics.

00:05:20.351 --> 00:05:23.120
The fit is amazingly good

00:05:23.787 --> 00:05:29.226
and shows that we really do understand
what’s happening in the young universe.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.864
In fact, the task of matching
those computer calculations to the data

00:05:33.897 --> 00:05:38.502
basically measures quite accurately
many of the fundamental properties

00:05:38.535 --> 00:05:40.037
of our universe.

00:05:40.604 --> 00:05:45.676
OK, let's now listen to the sound
after up-shifting by 50 octaves

00:05:45.709 --> 00:05:47.111
so that we can hear it.

00:05:47.544 --> 00:05:50.080
Now I’ve also used
those computer calculations

00:05:50.114 --> 00:05:52.816
to track the sound forward in time,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.720
starting at the Big Bang
and spanning the full acoustic era,

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.490
which ends at 400,000 years.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:04.895
The sound is accompanied
by a graphic that's a bit busy,

00:06:04.895 --> 00:06:07.030
but just focus on the green line,

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.533
which tracks the changing harmonics

00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:13.337
as the young universe
begins to expand and age.

00:06:13.637 --> 00:06:15.939
Also, don't worry,

00:06:15.939 --> 00:06:20.711
I’ve compressed those 400,000
years into just 10 seconds.

00:06:20.744 --> 00:06:22.045
(Laughter)

00:06:22.079 --> 00:06:23.280
Here we go.

00:06:34.925 --> 00:06:36.093
OK, thank you.

00:06:36.093 --> 00:06:39.763
(Applause)

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:46.170
If you weren't quite awake
before that, you are now.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:48.005
Well as I warned you,

00:06:48.038 --> 00:06:51.708
those broad harmonics make a sound
that's more like noise

00:06:51.708 --> 00:06:53.177
than music to our ears.

00:06:53.177 --> 00:06:55.345
And I'll return to that in just a minute.

00:06:55.345 --> 00:06:57.815
You obviously noticed the drop in pitch,

00:06:57.815 --> 00:06:59.483
and that's because as time passes,

00:06:59.516 --> 00:07:03.220
larger and larger regions,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:04.888
larger organ pipes,

00:07:04.888 --> 00:07:06.723
have had time to start sounding.

00:07:06.723 --> 00:07:08.992
And as their larger waves get included,

00:07:09.026 --> 00:07:11.628
also the overall pitch drops.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.432
Now you might wonder whether

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.937
this wonderful acoustic period
back in the universe’s youth

00:07:19.937 --> 00:07:25.309
left any traces in today's old universe
filled with galaxies.

00:07:25.442 --> 00:07:27.911
And the answer is yes, it has.

00:07:27.911 --> 00:07:29.313
And here's why.

00:07:29.313 --> 00:07:30.581
At 400,000 years,

00:07:30.614 --> 00:07:33.984
the cooling universe
suddenly turned transparent.

00:07:33.984 --> 00:07:37.988
The sound waves ceased
oscillating and froze in place,

00:07:37.988 --> 00:07:41.658
and the matter began to collapse
under its own gravity

00:07:41.692 --> 00:07:44.962
to make first stars and then galaxies.

00:07:45.262 --> 00:07:48.298
Now here's a map of today's old universe,

00:07:48.398 --> 00:07:51.301
spanning four billion light-years,
with us at the center.

00:07:51.335 --> 00:07:55.305
And it shows the positions
of about 100,000 galaxies.

00:07:55.672 --> 00:07:58.876
And if you perform
a similar wave analysis,

00:07:58.942 --> 00:08:01.812
sure enough, there's
the fundamental and harmonics

00:08:01.812 --> 00:08:04.815
showing up faintly
in those web-like patterns.

00:08:04.815 --> 00:08:09.353
The sound waves have turned
to stone, so to speak,

00:08:09.353 --> 00:08:13.056
and become fossilized
in the patterns of galaxies.

00:08:13.457 --> 00:08:15.792
But even more remarkable, I think,

00:08:15.792 --> 00:08:19.897
is that we think all
of this cosmic structure,

00:08:19.897 --> 00:08:22.032
the sound waves and the galaxy patterns,

00:08:22.132 --> 00:08:24.268
had their ultimate cause

00:08:24.268 --> 00:08:29.940
with subatomic quantum vibrations
during the universe's birth process.

00:08:30.107 --> 00:08:33.577
An incredibly short time
of hyperexpansion

00:08:33.644 --> 00:08:35.012
that we call inflation.

00:08:35.445 --> 00:08:38.515
Now this is a truly stunning proposition.

00:08:39.116 --> 00:08:42.653
That quantum oscillations
smaller than atoms

00:08:42.753 --> 00:08:44.955
get amplified by cosmic expansion

00:08:45.222 --> 00:08:47.791
to make huge sound waves

00:08:47.791 --> 00:08:51.361
that then transform

00:08:51.361 --> 00:08:54.898
into an ever-expanding
tapestry of galaxies.

00:08:54.898 --> 00:08:56.934
It's a direct connection

00:08:56.934 --> 00:08:59.937
between the quantum world
and the cosmic world.

00:09:01.605 --> 00:09:05.876
Well lastly, I wanted
to introduce a project

00:09:05.876 --> 00:09:09.546
that brings this primordial sound
and its changing harmonics

00:09:10.147 --> 00:09:13.317
into our own human musical world.

00:09:13.417 --> 00:09:17.788
And so after up-shifting by 50 octaves,

00:09:17.821 --> 00:09:22.559
I timed when the pitch of each harmonic
matched the pitch of each note

00:09:22.859 --> 00:09:26.129
on a simple piano keyboard,

00:09:26.129 --> 00:09:28.665
and you can see the notes here,
descending over time.

00:09:28.665 --> 00:09:33.003
Now this modifies the sound
in two important ways.

00:09:33.003 --> 00:09:36.406
It narrows those broad,
noise-like harmonics

00:09:36.406 --> 00:09:38.575
into single musical notes.

00:09:38.675 --> 00:09:43.213
And secondly, those notes get sounded
at particular times.

00:09:43.246 --> 00:09:47.985
So overall, the sound takes on
a more melodic and rhythmic character.

00:09:48.452 --> 00:09:51.521
So here's a brief
sonification of these notes

00:09:51.688 --> 00:09:57.260
for a period lasting 150,000 years,
compressed to 10 seconds.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:01.932
[Cosmic harmonics]

00:10:08.672 --> 00:10:09.840
OK, thank you.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.908
(Applause)

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:17.147
To finally add
artistic expression to this,

00:10:17.147 --> 00:10:22.119
I gave these notes
to sound artist Ander Mikalson,

00:10:22.119 --> 00:10:26.123
who worked with Pulitzer Prize-winning
composer Caroline Shaw

00:10:26.123 --> 00:10:29.860
to write a work for choir and organ,
lasting about 10 minutes,

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.229
and based on these note sequences.

00:10:32.729 --> 00:10:35.932
It's been performed a number of times,

00:10:35.932 --> 00:10:39.503
but here's a brief excerpt from
a performance in the Catholic Cathedral

00:10:39.503 --> 00:10:40.637
in Richmond, Virginia.

00:10:40.637 --> 00:10:44.474
[Music]

00:11:00.791 --> 00:11:05.062
(Applause)

00:11:09.166 --> 00:11:11.868
I think this is one of the few times

00:11:11.868 --> 00:11:14.204
I've experienced a genuine coming together

00:11:14.204 --> 00:11:16.540
of science and art,

00:11:16.573 --> 00:11:20.043
both framed within a sacred context.

00:11:20.043 --> 00:11:23.246
It was a moment to feel
deeply connected to

00:11:23.246 --> 00:11:25.949
and part of the entire universe.

00:11:27.117 --> 00:11:31.421
Those particles and atoms
from that first acoustic era

00:11:31.421 --> 00:11:33.824
went on to make stars

00:11:33.824 --> 00:11:36.660
and galaxies and planets
and, of course, us.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.396
And right now,

00:11:39.396 --> 00:11:41.798
some of those atoms are inside your brain,

00:11:41.798 --> 00:11:48.271
somehow witnessing and understanding
their own ancestry.

00:11:48.271 --> 00:11:51.274
They were out in that hot, glowing gas,

00:11:51.341 --> 00:11:53.977
participating in that first symphony.

00:11:53.977 --> 00:11:59.382
Nowhere else, as far as we know,
can the universe appreciate itself.

00:11:59.382 --> 00:12:03.553
The stars and galaxies
are not themselves sentient,

00:12:03.687 --> 00:12:04.821
but we are.

00:12:05.222 --> 00:12:09.126
We are an exceedingly
rare part of the universe.

00:12:09.493 --> 00:12:13.930
Perhaps the only part through which
the universe can witness

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:15.732
and understand

00:12:15.732 --> 00:12:18.368
and marvel at itself.

00:12:18.368 --> 00:12:19.669
Thank you.

00:12:19.736 --> 00:12:23.173
(Cheers and applause)

